<p>President Trump said he would fire Federal Reserve chair Jerome Powell if Powell did not leave “in time,” escalating a confrontation over leadership of the US central bank. He also signaled that he would not stop a Justice Department investigation tied to Powell. The Guardian reported that Trump has nominated Kevin Warsh to replace Powell, though a new chair would still need Senate approval. At the heart of the dispute is timing and authority. Powell’s term as Fed chair ends in May 2026, but multiple outlets reported that his separate term as a member of the Fed’s board of governors runs until 2028, meaning he could stay at the central bank after stepping down as chair. Trump’s latest remarks sharpen concerns about political pressure on the Fed at a moment when its independence and succession process are under close scrutiny.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/JWIVnVMfeidKS2nDpncdOFkkJY6FgTpphyznfXesvYg9y5bvYgOWvekJU-FjUm0P1hhG74_6mSBFSZ_hcCRSysXtVyuytehAyOtjjlH2yX_mE2gJqsQQOqMaMep9dEhCYMfJL0e-imn9TNQTG3Mdx0L0GcsSY5eKC8shSd60VrsQPuEIvlTM1lmLJaa5JzV7aIpib1jV-qBipyqg9m7iURVWmHjcw6JtdQabcUYP1gjSVRfMqQXWYvGBIB_wkkIOfKezZqdWS5BE3Wddvbs5' alt='President Trump and Federal Reserve chair Jerome Powell are at the center of a dispute over Fed leadership and timing.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Term split: Powell’s role as chair ends on 15 May 2026, but his separate Board of Governors term runs until 2028. That gap is at the center of the dispute over whether he could stay on at the Fed.</li><li>Named successor: Trump has put forward Kevin Warsh as his choice to lead the Federal Reserve, according to Guardian reporting on the administration’s succession plan.</li><li>Probe continues: Trump said he would not drop the Justice Department investigation involving Powell, widening the clash beyond interest-rate criticism into legal and institutional pressure.</li><li>Public warning: In a Fox Business interview, Trump said, “I’ve held back firing him” and added that Pow